=== SECRETS: environment variable vs file-based secret ===

############################################################
# 1. THE WRONG WAY: password in an environment variable
############################################################

--- starting a container with -e PASSWORD=<value> ---

--- docker inspect on that container (Env array) ---
    APP_NAME=demo
    POSTGRES_PASSWORD=DEMO-FAKE-PASSWORD-not-the-real-one-3f9a2c
    PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
[PASS] the password IS visible in docker inspect, in plain text

--- it also leaks to every process in the container ---
    /proc/1/environ:
      APP_NAME=demo
      POSTGRES_PASSWORD=DEMO-FAKE-PASSWORD-not-the-real-one-3f9a2c

    Anyone with access to the Docker socket can read this without entering
    the container, without any credential of their own, at any time while
    the container exists. It is also inherited by every child process and
    is commonly captured by crash reporters and process listings.

############################################################
# 2. THE RIGHT WAY: file-based Docker secret
############################################################

--- docker inspect on the REAL postgres container (Env array) ---
    POSTGRES_PASSWORD_FILE=/run/secrets/postgres_password
    POSTGRES_INITDB_ARGS=--encoding=UTF8 --locale=C
    POSTGRES_DB=appdb
    POSTGRES_USER=appuser
    PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin:/usr/lib/postgresql/16/bin
    GOSU_VERSION=1.19
    LANG=en_US.utf8
    PG_MAJOR=16
    PG_VERSION=16.15-1.pgdg13+2
    PGDATA=/var/lib/postgresql/data
[PASS] the real password does NOT appear anywhere in docker inspect
[PASS] only a PATH is exposed, not a value

--- where the value actually lives ---
    mount type and target inside the container:
      type=bind destination=/run/secrets/postgres_password rw=false
    file mode inside the container:
      -rwxrwxrwx root:root /run/secrets/postgres_password (41 bytes)

    The value is not in the container's config, so it is not in
    'docker inspect', not in the image, and not in any registry layer.

    HONEST LIMITATION: note 'type=bind' above. Compose (outside Swarm)
    implements file-based secrets as a BIND MOUNT of a host file, not as
    the in-memory tmpfs that Swarm/Kubernetes secrets use. Consequences:
      - the plaintext exists on the host disk at secrets/postgres_password.txt
      - the mode shown (777 here) is an artefact of the Windows/WSL bind
        mount, not a permission the file really carries on an ext4 host
      - it is still strictly better than an env var, because it stays out
        of container metadata, out of child process environments and out
        of crash dumps
      - for production, the host file should be sourced from a real secret
        manager (Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, SOPS-encrypted at rest) rather
        than generated and left on disk

